Add middleware in `app.js` after `sessionUser` to log HTTP traffic. A simple approach:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
  });
  next();
});
```

For database logging, wrap `db.query()` and `db.write()` to log the SQL and execution time. This catches every database operation without touching the route handlers.

For error logging, add error middleware at the end of `app.js`:

```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(`Error: ${err.message}`);
  res.status(500).end();
});
```

This approach keeps logging orthogonal to route logic—routes stay clean and logging is centralized.

Add a logging middleware in app.js before the route handlers.

Express middleware runs in order, so insert it after `sessionUser` middleware but before the route handlers:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    const elapsed = Date.now() - start;
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${elapsed}ms user:${req.user?.id}`);
  });
  next();
});
```

This logs once the response is sent, capturing method, path, status, response time, and the authenticated user. Place it after `sessionUser` so `req.user` is populated, and before the route handlers so it wraps all routes.

For production, replace `console.log` with a structured logger (winston, pino) and log request body/response for important operations, or use morgan for standard HTTP logging.
